73":"sling","H5414":"give","H2336":"thistle","H5927":"rise","H7910":"drunken","H7227":"many","H2342":"tremble","H3605":"all","H7936":"to hire","H5674":"to pass","H3611":"dog","H7725":"return","H5921":"upon","H6892":"vomit","H8138":"to change","H7200":"see","H376":"man","H8615":"cord","H4480":"from","H559":"to say","H6102":"sluggish","H7826":"lion","H1870":"conduct","H738":"lion","H996":"between","H7339":"street/plaza","H1817":"door","H5437":"turn","H6735":"envoy","H4296":"bed","H2934":"to hide","H6747":"dish","H3811":"be weary","H413":"to(wards)","H7651":"seven","H2940":"taste","H2388":"strengthen","H241":"ear","H7379":"strife","H3856":"to languish","H3384":"to shoot","H2131":"missile","H2671":"arrow","H4194":"death","H7411":"to shoot","H853":"[Obj.]","H7453":"neighbor","H7832":"to laugh","H589":"I","H657":"end","H6086":"wood","H3518":"to quench","H784":"fire","H369":"nothing","H5372":"whisperer","H8367":"be quiet","H4066":"strife","H6352":"coal","H1513":"coal","H4079":"contention","H2787":"to scorch","H3859":"to swallow","H1992":"they(masc.)","H3381":"to go down","H2315":"chamber","H990":"abdomen","H3701":"money","H5509":"dross","H6823":"to overlay","H2789":"earthenware","H8193":"lips","H1814":"to burn/pursue","H3820":"heart","H7451":"harmful","H5234":"to recognize","H8130":"to hate","H7130":"among","H7896":"make","H4820":"deceit","H3588":"for","H2603":"be gracious","H6963":"voice","H539":"be faithful","H8441":"abomination","H3680":"to cover","H8135":"hating","H4860":"guile","H1540":"Heglam","H6951":"assembly","H3738":"to dig","H7845":"hell","H5307":"fall","H1556":"to roll","H3956":"tongue","H8267":"deception","H1790":"crushed","H2509":"smooth","H6213":"do","H4072":"ruin"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["challoner",2,null,"challoner","As a bird, etc.... The meaning is, that a curse uttered without cause shall do no harm to the person that is cursed, but will return upon him that curseth, as whithersoever a bird flies, it returns to its own nest."],["challoner",4,null,"challoner","Answer not a fool, etc.... Viz., so as to imitate him but only so as to reprove his folly."],["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Glory and power. A fool in a high office will endanger himself and the public; (Calmet) while the virtuous, seeing that merit is not regarded, will not push themselves forward. (Ęschines.)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","As a bird, &c. The meaning is, that a curse uttered without cause shall do no harm to the person that is cursed, but will return upon him that curseth; as whithersoever a bird flies, it returns to its own nest. (Challoner) — Come. Chaldean, “shall not come in vain,” if it be just, like that of Noe, Josue, &c. Hebrew, “shall not come” (Calmet) to the person against whom it is uttered, though God will not hold the curser guiltless, as the Vulgate intimates. (Haydock) Curses, anathemas, &c., vented without reason, do not injure any but those who denounce them. Yet out of respect for ecclesiastical authority, those who are under censures, must abstain from their functions till they be absolved. (Calmet)"],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Snaffle. “Bit,” or muzzle, (camus) to prevent the animal from biting. (Haydock) — Septuagint, Arabic, &c., “a goad for an ass.” But metheg denotes a bridle. (Montanus; Haydock) asses being there very large, and commonly used for riding, chap. xiii. 13. (Calmet)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Answer not a fool, &c. Viz., so as to imitate him; but only so as to reprove his folly. (Challoner) — If thou answer at all, (ver. 5.) do it to the purpose, and to prevent others from taking scandal, (Calmet) as well as to humble the wicked, for his good. (Haydock)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Iniquity. He is exposed to shame, (Septuagint) and must condemn his own folly. (Calmet)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Fair. Hebrew, “unequal legs,” or “lifted up,” so, &c. (Haydock)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Mercury. The god of travellers, who were wont to throw a stone at the foot of his statue, as the Indians (Vincent. Bellor. xxiv.) and Arabs did. Mahomet would not disturb this superstitious custom. The Rabbins style these statues Mercolis. But Septuagint, &c., give another sense, “as he that bindeth a stone (Calmet) in the boss of a ring, sphendone, (Menochius) or in a sling,” can do no good, but only endanger himself or others, “so,” &c. Yet margema is never used elsewhere for a sling, and it means undoubtedly “a heap of stones,” (Calmet) as Montanus substitutes instead of “the sling,” in Pagnin. “As a small piece of precious stone in a heap of stones is lost, so,” &c. (Pole. Syn. Parkhurst in rogom.) (Haydock) — Honour, or an office, in which he may do harm. (Calmet)"],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","If. Hebrew, “as a thorn goeth up into the,” &c. (Protestants) (Haydock) — If he attempt to handle or to extract one, he will wound himself the more, as the fool would render truth and wisdom contemptible. (Calmet) — Parable. Septuagint, “but slavery in the hands of fools” groweth up. (Haydock)"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Anger, and prevent lawsuits. Hebrew is variously read and translated. (Calmet) — Protestants, “the great God, that formed all things, both rewardeth the fool and rewardeth the transgressors.” Marginal note, “a great man giveth all, and he hireth the fool,” &c. Septuagint, “all the flesh of fools is exposed to many storms, for their excess is punished.” (Haydock)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Dog. This is the only animal which is known to do so. St. Peter uses this comparison to deter any from renouncing the faith; as the Fathers do, to shew the misery attending a relapse. Septuagint here add, “there is a confusion,” &c., taken from Ecclesiasticus iv. 25. (Calmet)"],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Fool. The ignorant may be convinced that he wants instruction. (Calmet) — But “none are worse than the half-learned.” (Quintil. i. Jo. v. 21.)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Bed, which he will scarcely leave, though avarice push him forward. (St. Augustine, ser. xxii. or clxiv.)"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Seven, or many wise men, who used to speak in a sententious manner. (Calmet) — So seven is used, ver. 25."],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Anger. Hebrew, “passeth by, and meddleth with a quarrel not to him” pertaining. (Haydock) — Such expose themselves foolishly to danger. Great discretion is requisite to reconcile those at variance."],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","Jest. He will not escape, no more than the person who had committed murder involuntarily, if he were not in a city of refuge, Numbers xxxv. 22."],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","Faileth. Hebrew, Symmachus, Calmet, “aboundeth, the fire is bright, or flourisheth” (Septuagint) (Haydock)"],["haydock",23,null,"haydock","Dross. Hence the proud will be detested, and appear contemptible."],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","Lips. He will speak of what he hates much."],["haydock",26,null,"haydock","Deceitfully. Hebrew, “in desolation.” He will cringe, but when he finds an opportunity, he will discover his evil designs. (Calmet)"],["haydock",27,null,"haydock","Him. “Bad advice is worst to him who gives it.” (Varro. Rust. iii. 22.)"]],"au":{"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"],"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}